[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready recipe for loading a **single-file `.pmtiles` archive** into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

```bash
npm i pmtiles          # or yarn add pmtiles
```

`pmtiles` ships a tiny protocol handler (`new pmtiles.Protocol().tile`) that makes a `pmtiles://` URL work with MapLibre.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Create the protocol object
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the scheme name "pmtiles"
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

> **Why “once”?**  
> The protocol registry is global. Registering it a second time or inside a component’s render loop can cause duplicate handlers or memory leaks.

> **MapLibre v4+ note** – `pmtilesProtocol.tile` already contains the compatibility shim that works with the new promise-based `addProtocol` signature, so you don’t have to write your own async handler.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the archive

### Vector tiles (most common)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // One source per .pmtiles file
      world: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The protocol handler reads the archive header and returns a TileJSON
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/world.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw the "water" layer that lives inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'world',
        'source-layer': 'water',          // exact layer name inside the file
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },
      // Add more layers (roads, labels, etc.) that reference the same source
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'world',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

### Raster tiles (imagery)

```js
{
  sources: {
    imagery: {
      type: 'raster',
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/satellite.pmtiles',
      tileSize: 256               // optional, defaults to 256
    }
  },
  layers: [
    {
      id: 'satellite',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'imagery',
      paint: {}
    }
  ]
}
```

### Raster-DEM (terrain)

```js
{
  sources: {
    terrain: {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation.pmtiles',
      encoding: 'terrarium'      // or "mapbox"
    }
  },
  terrain: {
    source: 'terrain',
    exaggeration: 1.5
  }
}
```

> **Important:** Use **`url:`** (not `tiles:`). The PMTiles protocol reads the archive header, extracts the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom` and returns a TileJSON automatically. If you use a `tiles:` template you lose that information and MapLibre will assume a default `maxzoom: 22`, causing unnecessary 404s at higher zooms.

---

## 4️⃣ Host the `.pmtiles` file where it can serve **HTTP Range** requests

| Provider | What you need to set |
|----------|----------------------|
| **AWS S3** | Public read (or signed URL) + `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. Set a long `Cache-Control` (e.g. `public, max-age=31536000`). |
| **Cloudflare R2** | Same as S3; optionally put behind Cloudflare CDN. |
| **GitHub Pages** | File must be ≤ 100 MiB (the limit for raw files). |
| **Netlify / Vercel / any static host** | Must support `Range` and CORS (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`). |

If you get “CORS header ‘Access-Control-Allow-Origin’ missing” in the console, add the header on the bucket or CDN.

---

## 5️⃣ (Optional) Clean-up when the map is destroyed

```js
map.on('remove', () => {
  maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
});
```

Only needed if you dynamically create/destroy many maps in a single page; otherwise you can leave the protocol registered for the lifetime of the page.

---

## 6️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // 1️⃣ Register protocol
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Create map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser, and you should see the water polygons from the `world.pmtiles` archive.

---

## 7️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank vector tiles** (no features) | Using `tiles:` instead of `url:` or the archive’s `maxzoom` is lower than the map’s zoom. | Switch to `url:`; verify the archive’s `maxzoom` with `pmtiles show file.pmtiles`. |
| **CORS error** | Host does not send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. | Add the header on the bucket/CDN (or serve from same origin). |
| **404 on tile request** | Host does not support `Range` requests. | Use a host that does (S3, R2, Netlify, etc.) or enable the feature. |
| **“Tile fetch error: …”** after upgrading MapLibre to v4+ | Custom protocol still uses the old callback signature. | Use `pmtilesProtocol.tile` (it contains the v3-compat shim) or rewrite your handler to `async (params, abort) => ({data: …})`. |
| **File > 100 MiB on GitHub Pages** | GitHub Pages limits raw files to 100 MiB. | Host the file elsewhere (S3, R2, Cloudflare) and reference that URL. |

