## 8️⃣ Quick cheat-sheet

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Register once
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile);

// Map definition
new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      myData: {
        type: 'vector',               // or 'raster' / 'raster-dem'
        url: 'pmtiles://https://cdn.example.com/myData.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'my-layer',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myData',
        'source-layer': 'my_layer_name', // exact name inside the archive
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#ff6600' }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 3
});
```

That’s it—your `.pmtiles` archive is now a first-class tile source, with no tile server required. Happy mapping!

## What changed in MapLibre GL JS v4?

`maplibregl.addProtocol()` used to accept a **callback-style** handler:

```js
// v3 (and earlier)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))   // ← callback receives the raw data
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

Starting with **v4** the API became **promise-based** and the handler must return an **object that contains a `data` property** (the raw tile bytes). The second argument is now an **`AbortController`** instead of a callback, and the return value is no longer a `{ cancel }` object.

If you keep the old callback-style code, MapLibre receives a promise that resolves to `undefined`, so it thinks the tile has no data and you get blank tiles (or a silent failure).

---

## How to fix it

### 1. Convert the handler to the new signature

```js
// ✅ v4+ (and works on v5/v6 as well)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  // `params` contains: { url, type, ... }
  // `abortController.signal` can be passed to fetch so the request can be cancelled
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing aborts the tile request and makes MapLibre show an error tile
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  // You can return any of the following as the `data` value:
  //   - ArrayBuffer
  //   - Uint8Array / Buffer
  //   - Blob
  //   - Response (the whole Response object)
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // The contract requires the object `{ data: … }`.  Optional fields:
  //   cacheControl – string that will be used for the tile’s Cache-Control header
  //   expires      – Date or epoch ms for the Expires header
  return { data: buffer };
});
```

**Key points**

| Old (v3)                              | New (v4+)                                   |
|---------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| `(params, callback)`                  | `(params, abortController)`                |
| Call `callback(err, data)`            | `throw err` for failure, `return {data}`   |
| Return `{ cancel: … }`                | No return needed (the promise resolves)    |
| No `data` wrapper                     | Must return an object with a `data` field   |
| No abort support                      | Pass `abortController.signal` to `fetch`   |

### 2. If you already use the **PMTiles** library, you don’t need to rewrite anything

The `pmtiles` package ships a **compatibility shim** that adapts its own handler (`protocol.tile`) to both the old and the new API. All you have to do is:

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Register once, before any map is created
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);
```

That line works on v3 **and** v4+ because `protocol.tile` detects whether the second argument is a callback or an `AbortController` and returns the correct shape.

> **Tip:** If you are on a very old `pmtiles` version (< 3.0.0) it still uses the callback-only signature. Upgrade the npm package (`npm i pmtiles@latest`) and the shim will handle the v4 change for you.

### 3. Register the protocol **once** (global registry)

The protocol registry is global. Register it at application start-up (e.g. in the entry file of a React app, or before you create the first `new maplibregl.Map`). Registering it inside a component that mounts/unmounts repeatedly can cause duplicate registrations or memory leaks.

```js
// src/initMapLibre.js (or similar)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

export function initMapLibre() {
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);
}
```

Call `initMapLibre()` once, then create maps normally.

### 4. Clean-up (optional but recommended)

If you ever need to destroy the protocol (e.g. hot-module-replacement, test teardown), use the matching `removeProtocol`:

```js
maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
```

---

## Full working example (vector PMTiles)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles (v4)</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // ---- Register the PMTiles protocol (once) ----
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // ---- Create the map ----
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // The URL can be a plain HTTPS URL or the pmtiles:// wrapper.
        // Using `url:` makes MapLibre read the TileJSON from the archive header.
        osm: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/monaco.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
        }
        // …add more layers as you like
      ]
    },
    center: [7.4246, 43.7384],
    zoom: 13
  });

  // Optional: clean up when the page unloads
  window.addEventListener('unload', () => {
    map.remove();
    maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the example does

1. **Imports** MapLibre v4 and the latest `pmtiles` library.
2. **Registers** the `pmtiles://` protocol using the built-in shim (`protocol.tile`).  
   No callback code, no abort handling – the shim does it for you.
3. **Creates** a map whose vector source points at a `.pmtiles` file via `url:` (the recommended way; MapLibre reads the min/max zoom from the archive header automatically).
4. **Adds** a couple of layers that reference different `source-layer`s inside the same PMTiles file.

---

## If you *must* write your own custom protocol (non-PMTiles)

Sometimes you want to serve tiles from a bespoke backend that does **not** use the PMTiles format. The same pattern applies:

```js
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortController) => {
  // Example: a tile server that expects /{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
  const url = `https://my-backend.example.com/tiles/${params.z}/${params.x}/${params.y}.pbf`;

  const response = await fetch(url, { signal: abortController.signal });
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Tile ${params.z}/${params.x}/${params.y} missing`);
  }

  // Return the raw bytes wrapped in {data}
  const arrayBuffer = await response.arrayBuffer();
  return { data: arrayBuffer };
});
```

**Remember**:

* **Never** return the raw `ArrayBuffer` alone – it must be inside `{ data: … }`.
* Throw an `Error` (or any exception) to signal a failure; MapLibre will treat it as a missing tile.
* Use `abortController.signal` so that MapLibre can cancel in-flight requests when the user pans/zooms quickly.

---

## Quick checklist for the migration

| ✅ Done? | Item |
|--------|------|
| ☐ | Replace every `addProtocol('name', (params, callback) => { … })` with an **async** function that returns `{ data: … }`. |
| ☐ | Pass `abortController.signal` to any `fetch`/XHR you perform. |
| ☐ | Throw on HTTP errors instead of calling `callback(err)`. |
| ☐ | If you only need PMTiles, **remove** your custom handler and use `pmtiles.Protocol().tile` (upgrade the `pmtiles` npm package if needed). |
| ☐ | Register the protocol **once**, before any `new maplibregl.Map()` runs. |
| ☐ | If you ever call `removeProtocol`, do it *after* all maps that use the protocol have been destroyed. |
| ☐ | Verify that the host serving the tiles returns `Accept-Ranges: bytes` and proper CORS headers (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*`). |
| ☐ | Test a few zoom levels; you should see vector data instead of blank tiles. |

---

## TL;DR code snippet (minimal fix)

```js
// Old v3 code (doesn't work on v4+)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});

// New v4+ code
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  const resp = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`Tile error ${resp.status}`);
  const buf = await resp.arrayBuffer();
  return { data: buf };          // <-- the required wrapper
});
```

Replace the old block with the new one (or, if you’re using PMTiles, just swap in the `pmtiles.Protocol().tile` shim) and your custom tiles will work again under MapLibre GL JS v4, v5, and v6. Happy mapping!
